Thoracic Outlet Syndrome
Musculoskeletal & Pain
Ulnar distribution paresthesia, Positive Roos/Adson's tests, Symptoms with overhead elevation

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: MKP-061
Reason for Visit: Acute, distressing exacerbation of severe upper extremity heaviness, pain, and profound numbness preventing sleep or basic arm use.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage identified a patient experiencing severe, unremitting arm pain, profound heaviness, and vascular color changes following a period of sustained overhead activity. Urgent physical assessment was mandated to quickly differentiate acute neurovascular compression at the thoracic outlet from a central ischemic event or an acute deep vein thrombosis of the upper extremity, and to provide immediate mechanical offloading and neuropathic pain control before daytime services.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My whole arm feels dead and incredibly heavy; the pain is shooting down my neck into my pinky finger, and it's keeping me awake."
History of Present Illness: Patient presents with an acute, severe exacerbation of right upper extremity neurovascular symptoms. Timeline: Insidious history of aching pain and mild tingling over the past 3 months; drastically worsened 6 hours ago after the patient spent the afternoon painting a ceiling. Currently reports an agonizing aching pain, profound heaviness, and severe fatigue involving the entire right upper extremity, neck, and ipsilateral shoulder. Patient describes profound paresthesia and numbness distinctly localizing to the ulnar nerve distribution (little and ring fingers). There is a marked exacerbation of both neurological and vascular symptoms during any sustained overhead arm elevation. The patient complains of the hand feeling uncomfortably cold with transient, mild purplish color changes when the arm is raised.
Functional Status: Highly impaired right upper limb; unable to perform any tasks requiring elevation or sustained grip due to profound fatigue and pain.
Social/Safety Concerns: Sleep deprivation and significant anxiety regarding the sensation of a "dead" arm.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 130/82 | HR: 88 | Temp: 36.7 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 75 Kg
General: Alert, distressed, resting the right arm across the lap to offload shoulder tension.
Physical Exam: Inspection reveals no gross muscle atrophy of the intrinsic hand muscles currently, but mild, diffuse, non-pitting swelling is noted in the right hand compared to the left. Exquisite focal tenderness palpated directly over the right supraclavicular fossa and hypertonic scalene triangle. Neurological exam confirms altered sensation strictly in the C8/T1 ulnar distribution. Distinctly positive Roos test: patient is unable to complete even 60 seconds of opening and closing the hands with arms abducted due to the immediate reproduction of severe ischemic pain, extreme heaviness, and severe paresthesia. Positive Adson's maneuver: a marked decrease of the right radial pulse is noted with deep inspiration and head rotation toward the affected side. Capillary refill is sluggish (3 sec) immediately following provocation but normalizes at rest.
Environmental Observations: Paint rollers and ladders visible, confirming the mechanism of prolonged overhead mechanical stress.
Medication Reconciliation: Took Paracetamol and Ibuprofen with zero relief of the deep neurogenic ache.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Acute flare of Thoracic Outlet Syndrome (Neurogenic predominant with mild vascular compression) - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Cervical Radiculopathy (C8/T1) - Ruled out clinically by the profound vascular/heaviness components, positive Roos/Adson's tests, and lack of true isolated motor weakness in the hand intrinsic muscles on resting examination.

Upper Extremity DVT (Paget-Schroetter) - Unlikely given the rapid reversibility of the vascular changes with positioning and the prominent neuropathic pain, but requires formal ultrasound exclusion if swelling persists.

Ulnar Neuropathy at the Elbow (Cubital Tunnel) - Ruled out by the presence of severe neck/shoulder pain, supraclavicular tenderness, and positive thoracic outlet provocation tests, rather than isolated elbow pain and a positive Tinel's at the cubital tunnel.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with a severe, functionally limiting crisis of neurovascular compression at the thoracic outlet following classic mechanical overload (overhead work). The critical clinical imperative was to differentiate this complex syndrome from an acute cervical disc herniation or an upper extremity DVT. Physical examination definitively localized the dynamic compression to the scalene triangle/thoracic outlet via highly specific provocation maneuvers (Roos, Adson's) and confirmed the mixed neurogenic and vascular presentation. The severity of the symptoms rendered the arm useless and prevented sleep. Urgent after-hours assessment safely provided diagnostic reassurance, ruled out a permanent structural stroke or acute clot, and initiated targeted mechanical postural changes and neuropathic analgesia.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Prescribed oral Pregabalin 75mg Nocté to specifically target the severe neurogenic burning and paresthesia. Advised ongoing use of Ibuprofen 400mg TDS for scalene muscle inflammation.
Positive Behavioral Change:
Action: Sleep with a thick pillow positioned under the right elbow and forearm to slightly elevate and support the arm while resting on the back. Consequence: Complete mechanical offloading of the heavy arm prevents it from dragging downward on the collarbone, immediately opening the thoracic outlet space and stopping the agonizing nerve compression (negative reinforcement).
Action: Perform slow, gentle "chin tuck" retractions (giving yourself a double chin) for 5 seconds every hour. Consequence: This actively stretches the tight, inflamed scalene muscles in the front of the neck, reducing the physical pressure they apply to the nerve bundle passing beneath them (positive reinforcement).
Home-Based Services (Optional): Mandatory referral to a specialized physical therapist for first-rib mobilization and postural strengthening.
DME/Safety (Optional): N/A
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 2 weeks to arrange diagnostic ultrasound (venous/arterial) and nerve conduction studies to fully map the extent of the compression.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate ED presentation if the arm suddenly turns completely blue, white, or freezing cold and does not recover when lowered, or if the entire arm swells massively.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is clinical, representing dynamic pinching of nerves and blood vessels between your collarbone and ribs. Formal ultrasound and nerve tests are required for a complete evaluation. Conditions are dynamic.
Clinical Course: The acute numbness and heaviness will improve significantly over 48 hours by strictly avoiding overhead movement and supporting the arm. Permanent resolution relies entirely on highly specific physiotherapy to correct shoulder posture.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek emergency medical care if your entire arm suddenly turns blue or white and stays cold, or if it swells up massively like a balloon.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The main bundle of nerves and blood vessels traveling to your arm is being severely crushed between your collarbone and neck muscles. The overhead painting caused those neck muscles to spasm and swell, closing the space completely. The nerve medication will quiet the severe electrical burning.
Actionable Steps: Absolutely no overhead lifting or reaching. When sitting or sleeping, you must support the weight of your arm on a pillow. If the arm hangs unsupported, gravity will drag your shoulder down and crush the nerves further.

